4. Changes in 3.1
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
As some of you have undoubtedly discovered, the Dev Team has made *extensive* 
changes in version 3.1.  This is a brief overview of some of the many 
changes, and includes screen shots of several of the new levels:

1) Many new items have been added to the game, including candles, loads of 
new artifacts (named weapons), and several new special items that are 
instrumental in winning the game.

2) Quite a few new monsters have been added to the game.  The most memorable 
is the Mind Flayer that I ran across the other day.  Humanoid monsters are 
also genderized now.  (I.E. instead of "it hits," "he hits" or "she hits.")

3) Intelligent monsters now can do pretty much anything that you can.  This 
includes things like picking up and wielding weapons, quaffing potions or 
throwing them at you, picking up and zapping wands at you, and much much 
more.  A basic rule that becomes very important is this:  RUN towards any 
wands that you see.  If some gnome picks it up and it's a wand of lightning, 
you're TOAST! 

4) Virtually all of the methods of robbing a shop have been eliminated.  
Going quickly through the methods listed in the WCST Spoilers, we find that 
the following has transpired:  

  A) Teleporting stuff out works.  Unfortunately, the shopkeeper charges you 
     full price for anything you teleport out. 
  B) If you dig in a shop wall or in the shop floor, two things happen:  
     first, the shopkeeper attacks you immediately, and second, any holes you 
     manage to dig "heal" themselves after a couple of rounds.  
  C) Polymorphing the shopkeeper still works.  However, it's still risky, as 
     the shopkeeper may resist the polymorph.
  D) Training your pet to steal still works.
  E) Teleporting out still works, but it still generates Keystone Kops.  I 
     haven't checked to see if it's still possible to trap the shopkeeper in 
     the shop with a boulder. 

5) Polymorphing has changed somewhat.  Now, just as you have a chance of 
system shock when you polymorph yourself, there is a chance that when you try 
to polymorph another creature it will die of system shock.  Something similar 
to system shock has also been added for items:  Each time you polymorph an 
item, there is a chance that it will just disintegrate.  Additionally, when 
polymorphing a pile of items, the pile may partially shuffle itself 
(eliminating the strategy of piling things in order of plusses), or several 
items may combine to form a golem, which will promptly attack you.  In my 
mind, this effectively limits poly-piling, and brings it back into the realms 
of game balance.

6) Pets are now a lot easier to deal with.  You no longer have to worry about 
getting trapped in a long corridor when your pet walks in after you and then 
kills something which was carrying a cursed item.  The reason for this is 
simple:  If you attempt to move onto the spot where your pet is, you 
"displace" your pet, moving it to an adjacent square or switching places with 
it.  Bravo, Dev Team! 

7) Spellbooks are now a lot more useful and powerful.  You can read 
spellbooks multiple times, which in my mind makes a lot more sense.  There is 
still a chance of failure when reading a spellbook, though, and if you fail, 
your spellbook goes goodbye.

8) Appropriate interruptions have been added to the game.  In other words, 
you no longer have to worry about things like starving to death because you 
hit 300 and then the period key when you were weak.

9) A nicer user interface has been added for things like bags and chests 
which can hold other things (thank goodness!).

10) Kicking chests, sacks, etc., can now break things inside, so be careful 
about randomly bashing chests open.

11) Hallucinating is now a lot more fun (if that's possible).  Whereas in 3.0 
when you were hallucinating the game would randomly make things look like 
other things in the game and make objects look like other objects in the 
game, now they've added a whole separate list of things that you can see only 
when hallucinating.  For instance, you can get hit by the Ravenous Bugblatter 
Beast of Traal now, or even be attacked by the Borg.  I only hope that the 
Dev Team has done a good job on researching copyright and trademark laws so 
that we don't end up losing Nethack in a stupid lawsuit.

12) Several spells now have area effects.  The most notable of these is 
fireball, where an area will erupt in flame, possibly killing multiple 
creatures, including yourself.

13) When using ESP (i.e. after you've eaten a floating eye), watch out:  
Mindless undead such as zombies and skeletons no longer show up, because they 
have no mind to detect with ESP.  Vampires and such will still be visible 
with ESP, though.

As you've surely read in the readme file, the game can now split off into 
side levels (I.E., there may be two stairways down on a level leading to two 
different levels).  There are also magic portals that can transport you to 
special levels.  Before I show you the levels, two notes:  a) on several of 
the levels shown there are a number of secret doors that I have not shown on 
the map -- I leave these to you and your imagination to discover; and b) I 
show the levels relatively clear of monsters and items (except for important 
ones) -- this doesn't mean that the level won't be littered with them!  Now 
then, hre's a rundown of the new levels:

1) The Mines:  The mines split off from the main levels at a relatively low 
level.  You can tell that you're in the mines when you start running into an 
inordinately large number of gnomes, which populate the mines in great 
quantities.

2) The Mine Town Level:  In the mines, you will find a level that everybody 
has been praying for and asking for for quite a long time.  It is basically a 
"mall" level, and includes several shops and a temple.  A sample mine town 
level is shown below.  Note that the central area is constant, although the 
contents of the various rooms is not (I.E. which rooms are shops, and what 
the shops sell changes).  There are also a number of town guards that keep 
the peace.  Additionally, the rest of the level, other than the central area, 
is random. 

